INT. HIGH SCHOOL ROOFTOP GREENHOUSE - DAWN

A glass lean-to bolted to the school's flat roof. Trays of tomato seedlings under grow-lights. Condensation beads the panes. A broken radio sits unplugged on a workbench. Nobody is here yet.

A key rattles in the lock. The door swings open. TESSA (17, thrift-store coat, messy braid, a backpack heavy with textbooks) steps in, shoulders hunched, and shuts out the wind.

She drops her bag. Cups her hands and breathes into them. Crosses to the seedlings and touches one leaf, gently, like a held breath.

She pulls out her phone. Dials. Puts it to her ear. It rings once, twice, three times. Click.

TESSA (V.O.)
(recorded, cheerful, younger)
Hi, you've reached Tessa. I'm probably playing outside. Leave a message and I'll call you back!

She closes her eyes. Holds the phone away. Listens to her own voice, tinny and small. She smiles, then stops smiling.

TESSA
(to the plants)
Four years ago. I was eleven. I thought I'd be famous.

She hangs up. Dials again. The recording plays again. She doesn't smile this time. When it ends, she listens to the beep, mouth open, as if there might be something to say, and says nothing.

A sparrow lands on the glass above her. She looks up. It sits there, puffed against the cold, watching.

She sets the phone on the bench. Picks up a watering can. Fills it at the spigot. Tilts it over the seedlings. Water patters in the quiet. Outside, the first bus shudders into the lot below, and the world beyond the glass begins its ordinary morning.

TESSA (CONT'D)
Okay. Okay. Let's go.
